      "id": "letterboxd-review-1381509851",
      "medium": "film",
      "mlabel": "Film",
      "title": "A Tribe Called Love",
      "year": "2025",
      "rating": 4,
      "rewatch": false,
      "date": "4 Jul",
      "watchedDate": "2026-07-04",
      "reviewTitle": "A Tribe Called Love",
      "review": "Warra a watch. I think this is one of the most deeply personal experiences I felt with a movie in a longtime. The people I talked to after the film itself, the messaging. The film really did highlights the problems we see plaguing our community, in such a beautiful and comedic way. I remember speaking to a fellow peer of mine that watched the movie as well, and then I asked them, \"Hey, what do you think about the movie?\" We're casually talking about some of the plot points that were devastating and are actually impacting our lives as well, like gun violence in that aspect. It just shows how prevalent the issue is really. One personal message the director mentioned during Q&A that really stuck with me was the stubborn nature of Somali men and how, in our ways, we tend to harm ourselves or kill ourselves in the process rather than coming together. I'm just super excited for Somali cinema in general. I think seeing this film and the production level, the quality, the acting, I'm just excited for what's coming next, the next wave. I'm ngl, this might be up there for sure, especially for Somali cinema. This is the bar to beat. The animation was superb. Just wowed me; seemed like a personal love letter to Somalia and Toronto. The timeline on this was just nuts: 23 days, 8 hours a day of shooting. Just hearing this is just top-notch stuff, man. I can't wait to see all the other stuff from Mawimbi and Mohammed himself, Phenomenal Film. If you’re an Aspiring SOMALI CINEPHILE PICKUP THE CAMERA and shoot something, LIKE MOHAMED said. 🩵🇸🇴",
